Hmmm...that's what I get for trusting the *comments* in the source,
rather than testing in wizmode.  It should be impossible (according to
the comments in worn.c L351 and L378) for a monster to put on a shirt
when it's already wearing armor.  However, there's a bug (|| rather
than && in L379) which makes this check fail.  I was puzzling over
that operator, but for some reason decided to trust it did what it was
supposed to.  I wonder if this is a known/fixed bug...

They won't actually take the armor off first, though.  They'll fake
taking a cloak off to put on a shirt/armor under it (by taking longer
than otherwise), but the shirt just magically goes on under the rest
for free.  (Amusingly, those comments are specific that this behavior
would be "too obviously buggy...")

Please read the above as [Monsters are not supposed to be able to put
on a shirt if wearing a suit of armor, but can due to a bug in 3.4.3].

>> Suits:   Only MZ_MEDIUM humanoids (excluding winged gargoyles and
>>          mariliths) which are not whirly and not incorporeal.
>>          Additionally, some monsters have a racial exception allowing
>>          them to wear certain suits (currently only hobbits able to
>>          wear any elven armor)
>
> _Elves_ can wear elven armor, if I remember correctly.

_Elves_ are size MZ_HUMAN (which is the same as MZ_MEDIUM; I should
have said that when I wrote the above).  They can wear elven armor for
the normal reason that they're the right size for it, humanoid, have
hands, are intelligent, etc.  They can also wear any other suit of
armor they come across which is better.  (In other words, they don't
need the racial exception.)

_Hobbits_ are size MZ_SMALL, and would thus ordinarily be unable to
wear body armor.  However, they get a special exception in the code to
be able to wear any elven armor.  The comments suggest this has
something to do with a famous hobbit being on record as having done
so.

